  "summary": "Walter Kim's medication list now matches his St. Brigid discharge papers (summary plus signed addendum, 10/19), except for three items that need your decision.\n\n**Changes made:**\n- **Furosemide:** increased from 20 mg to 40 mg daily. I modified the old order, which stopped it and created a new order for the 40 mg tablet (mr-t0001).\n- **Potassium chloride:** stopped, as the discharge summary directs.\n\n**No change needed:**\n- **Metoprolol XL 50 mg** in the discharge list is another name for his metoprolol succinate ER 50 mg daily order.\n- **Atorvastatin and aspirin:** continued as before.\n- **Omeprazole 20 mg** stays. The hospital gave pantoprazole only as a formulary substitute, so I didn't order it.\n\n**Needs your decision:**\n- **Lisinopril and spironolactone:** neither has been restarted or started. His potassium on 10/21 was 5.4 (high), and RBC-TOC-02 §7 doesn't allow starting or restarting potassium-raising drugs while it's above 5.0.\n  - Lisinopril stays on hold. The discharge condition for restarting it was creatinine below 1.5, and his 10/21 creatinine was 1.32, so the potassium is the only thing holding it back.\n  - Spironolactone was not ordered. The addendum corrected the dose to 25 mg daily (the summary said 12.5 mg).\n  - I created a task for you (task-t0003), due 10/26, to repeat a potassium or basic metabolic panel and then decide on both drugs. I didn't order the repeat lab myself.\n- **Metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily:** the discharge papers don't mention it, so I left it unchanged. He's also on metoprolol succinate, so the two may be a double beta-blocker. I messaged you about it (comm-t0004).\n\nThe full reconciliation is documented in a signed progress note (doc-t0005).",
